      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\), we need to find the largest 4-digit integer \\( n \\) such that \\( n \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22} \\).\n\nFirst, identify the largest 4-digit integer. The largest 4-digit integer is 9999. We need to find the largest integer \\( n \\) such that \\( n \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22} \\). This means we need to find the largest \\( n \\) such that when \\( n \\) is divided by 22, the remainder is 15.\n\nWe can express this relationship as:\n\\[ n = 22k + 15 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). We need \\( n \\) to be a 4-digit number, so we solve for \\( k \\) such that:\n\\[ 1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\leq 9999 \\]\n\nFirst, solve the inequality for the lower bound:\n\\[ 1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\]\n\\[ 985 \\leq 22k \\]\n\\[ k \\geq \\frac{985}{22} \\]\n\\[ k \\geq 44.7727 \\]\nSince \\( k \\) must be an integer, we take the smallest integer greater than or equal to 44.7727, which is 45. So, \\( k = 45 \\).\n\nNow, substitute \\( k = 45 \\) back into the equation for \\( n \\):\n\\[ n = 22 \\cdot 45 + 15 \\]\n\\[ n = 990 + 15 \\]\n\\[ n = 1005 \\]\n\nThus, the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\) is \\(\\boxed{9999}\\).",
