      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\), we need to determine the largest 4-digit number and then adjust it to satisfy the given congruence condition.\n\nFirst, identify the largest 4-digit number:\n\\[\n9999\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the largest integer \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[\nx \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22}\n\\]\nThis means that \\( x \\) can be expressed in the form:\n\\[\nx = 22k + 15\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nWe need \\( x \\) to be less than or equal to 9999:\n\\[\n22k + 15 \\leq 9999\n\\]\nSubtract 15 from both sides:\n\\[\n22k \\leq 9984\n\\]\nDivide both sides by 22:\n\\[\nk \\leq \\frac{9984}{22}\n\\]\nCalculate the division:\n\\[\nk \\leq 453.8181\\ldots\n\\]\nSince \\( k \\) must be an integer, the largest possible value for \\( k \\) is 453.\n\nNow, substitute \\( k = 453 \\) back into the expression for \\( x \\):\n\\[\nx = 22 \\cdot 453 + 15\n\\]\nCalculate the product:\n\\[\n22 \\cdot 453 = 9966\n\\]\nAdd 15:\n\\[\nx = 9966 + 15 = 9981\n\\]\n\nThus, the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9981}\n\\]",
